\details{
\strong{Dates.} The service stores dates as milliseconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC. Each date field (\code{stormdate}, \code{surveydate}, \code{starttime}, \code{endtime},
\code{edit_time}, \code{created_date}, \code{last_edited_date}, as the layer has them) is kept as that number, and the column of the same name with the suffix \verb{_utc} is the same instant as
a date-time in UTC. The service gives the numbers as text; they are stored as numbers, with the same values. A missing date is \code{NA}.

\strong{Field values are the service's.} Fields such as \code{efscale} hold whatever the survey teams entered, including values that are not EF ratings (for example \code{EFU}, \code{TSTM/Wind}, \code{N/A}, \code{UNKNOWN});
\code{event_id} is free text (\code{data-raw/known_issues.md} gives how it is filled) and is not a key that links the layers or an NCEI event id.

\strong{Status of the data.} The data are preliminary: the stewards describe the dataset as preliminary and as intended for public access and use
(\url{https://disasters-geoplatform.hub.arcgis.com/pages/noaa-damage-assessment-toolkit-dat}). No license text was found, and this package does not claim that the data are in the public domain.
The accuracy of the damage polygons has not been measured, positions are the survey teams', and surveys are not available for every tornado:
coverage grows from a few tornadoes in 2007 to most tornadoes in 2025 (\code{data-raw/known_issues.md}).

\strong{Linking.} \link{tornado_dat_link} links the lines to SPC tornadoes and \link{dat_polygon_line_link} relates the polygons to the lines. Points are not linked to tornadoes by any rule.
